  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nForecast confidence is low for Wednesday night into Thursday on\nthe timing of rain showers and thunderstorms and any strong to\nsevere potential for thunderstorms. High resolution forecast \nmodel guidances are not in good agreement for how the weather \nsystems will move through eastern New York and western New \nEngland. \n\nFor Wednesday night, the current forecast supports a few rain \nshowers for locations north of the I-90 corridor as a weather \nsystem to our north moves southeast into New England bringing \nlow to medium (15-45%) chances for scattered rain showers \nthrough the overnight hours into the early morning hours \nThursday. \n\nFor Thursday, extensive cloud coverage and depending on the\ntrack of an upper level shortwave and surface low pressure\nsystem, we could see very limited ingredients needed for strong\nto severe storms to develop on Thursday afternoon. \nNevertheless, an isolated non-severe thunderstorm could develop\nduring the afternoon hours for any location that sees some \nclearing of the clouds. Scattered rain showers during the \nafternoon is currently supported by the latest forecast for\nThursday. As chances are low to medium at this forecast period,\nconfidence is higher for little to no impacts of flash flooding \nfor Thursday as ingredients needed for these impacts are not\npresent in forecast model guidances. Nevertheless, keep up to \ndate with the latest forecast as we get closer to Thursday and a\ndreary day is likely in store across eastern New York and \nwestern New England on Thursday due to overcast skies and the\nlow to medium chances for rain showers.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBased on latest forecast trends and ensemble forecast model \nguidances, below normal temperatures are in store for across eastern \nNew York and western New England for Friday through this weekend. \nNormal high temperatures for mid-August range in the low 80s(80-83 \ndegrees) to mid-70s in higher terrain locations. The latest forecast \nsupports high temperatures ranging in the upper 60s for high terrain \nlocations and mid-70s for elsewhere across eastern New York and \nwestern New England beginning Friday through the weekend. Confidence \nis high for temperatures to range 3 to 8 degrees below normal. For \nlow temperatures Saturday morning, surface high pressure is \nsupported to be overhead eastern New York and the southern \nAdirondacks could get very chilly with lows in the upper 30s to low \n40s. We'll keep an eye on the trend for the track of the surface \nhigh pressure if temperatures need to be adjusted for Saturday \nmorning below the deterministic model guidance. Latest probabilities \nfor the high terrain of the southern Adirondacks is high (greater \nthan 75 percent) for below 45 degrees. For those planning on camping \nand outdoor recreation this weekend for the southern Adirondacks, a \nchilly Saturday morning can be in store.",
